clearcote: SOCKS5 UDP relay (RFC 1928 section 7)

Chromium proxies TCP through SOCKS5 but never UDP, so with a proxy configured WebRTC either
bypasses it or does not flow. This adds a DatagramServerSocket that performs UDP ASSOCIATE and
frames every datagram, and hooks it into the one place P2P UDP sockets are built.

Three things this got wrong first and which the tests caught, recorded so they are not
reintroduced:

  1. SOCKS5ClientSocket::Connect() writes its greeting immediately -- it wraps a socket that
     SOCKSConnectJob has ALREADY connected. Handing it a fresh TCPClientSocket trips
     DCHECK(socket_) in TCPSocketPosix::Write(). The transport is now connected first.

  2. A datagram queued before the association came up completed on the wire but its caller
     never got a callback, so the send hung forever.

  3. The DST field means something DIFFERENT for UDP ASSOCIATE. For CONNECT it is the host to
     reach; RFC 1928 defines it for ASSOCIATE as the address the CLIENT will send from.
     Reusing the CONNECT builder declared the proxy own address, so the proxy accepted the
     association and then silently dropped every datagram -- danted logged the session as 0/0
     and the caller simply waited. Declaring 0.0.0.0:0 lets the proxy learn the source from
     the first datagram.

Listen() is synchronous and has no completion callback, so it binds the socket, starts the
association, and queues datagrams until the relay endpoint is known.

Opt-in via --socks5-udp. Both that switch and --proxy-server are forwarded to the network
service, where the P2P socket factory reads them -- without that the feature silently does
nothing, which is how the r14 auth work failed first time.

Only an IP-literal proxy is relayed; a hostname would need an async resolve the synchronous
factory cannot await, and declines to a direct socket rather than half-working.

Verified on 151 against a UDP-capable danted and a residential proxy that refuses ASSOCIATE:
datagram round-trip confirmed by the proxy own byte accounting (29/1 in, 19/1 forwarded,
24/1 back, 34/1 out), wrong-password and no-proxy both fail cleanly, and TCP CONNECT through
the same shared handshake still loads a page. Builds clean for Linux and Windows.
